045417] [17592186065093 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064230 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065094 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186065095 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065094 #edb/ref 1061 16962 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065094 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "42aa28fe-57c7-5ba0-9e1f-9bced6b58754" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065094 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186065364 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065095 #edb/ref 1061 16963 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065095 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "027aea00-6bd5-5235-9135-7e82295e6484" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065095 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064231 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065361 #edb/ref 1064 "Multiplying any number by zero *always* equals zero. Below are some examples.\n\n- $5 \\times 0 = 0$\n- $123\\times 0 = 0$\n- $-314\\,159 \\times 0 = 0$\n- $0\\times 0 = 0$\n\nTo see *why* multiplying any number by zero always equals zero, let's take a look at the multiplication table for $5$, shown below.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5 \\cdot 4 &= 20 \\\\\n5 \\cdot 3 &= 15 \\\\\n5 \\cdot 2 &= 10 \\\\\n5 \\cdot 1 &= 5 \\\\\n5 \\cdot 0 &= ◻\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs we go down the list, the number in blue is reduced by $5$ each time. Following this pattern, we must have\n\n$$\n\\color{blue}\\square = 0\n$$\n\n.\n\nNote that, since multiplication is commutative, multiplying zero by any number (including itself) equals zero as well. Some examples are shown below.\n\n- $0 \\times 5 = 0$\n- $0 \\times 123 = 0$\n- $0 \\times -314\\,159 = 0$\n- $0 \\times 0 = 0$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065361 #edb/ref 1065 3119 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065361 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "7ed778da-8d5a-5363-972b-d9435e22a0e1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065361 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065363 #edb/ref 1064 "Dividing zero by any number (other than zero) always equals zero. For example:\n\n- $0 \\div 2 = 0$\n- $0\\div (-456) = 0$\n\nTo understand why dividing zero by any number (other than zero) always equals zero, think about it this way: if you have zero pizzas, and you want to divide zero pizzas among ten friends, then each friend will get exactly zero pizza." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065363 #edb/ref 1065 3120 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065363 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "65c0e3ec-7962-5911-a3c9-9de126e87edc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065363 #edb/ref 1067 "Dividing Zero by Another Number" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065364 #edb/ref 1064 "Division by zero is **undefined.** No number can be divided by zero, not even zero itself.\n\nTo see why, notice that if we divide $6$ by $3$, we get\n\n$$\n6\\div 3 = 2\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the relationship between multiplication and division, we have\n\n$$\n6 = 3\\times 2\n$$\n\nHowever, this argument does not work if we divide by zero. Suppose we have\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nThen, by the relationship between multiplication and division, we have\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nand this relationship cannot be true because any number multiplied by zero is zero! Therefore, $$? is not a number." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065364 #edb/ref 1065 3121 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065364 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "d8152649-f215-53b2-9811-ce74f0d61dc3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065364 #edb/ref 1067 "Dividing by Zero is Undefined" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
